### Analyzer key-reader fix (ground-truth tested, not web research)
Reproduced defect: the shipped key reader named keys on non-music — white noise read "A minor" (0.63–0.67 Krumhansl correlation), pink noise read "F major" (0.87) — writing an invented tonal center into generated prompts. Root causes measured: magnitude-summed chroma (broadband energy mimics profiles), FFT bin-count bias across pitch classes (21 bins vote C vs 9 for C# in the 55–4200 Hz band), and no tonalness check. Fix in `client/src/analyze.ts`: chroma accumulates power (mag²) with per-frame normalization; a bin-count bias shape is subtracted before correlation; a spectral-flatness gate (median evidence frame ≤ 0.30; tonal ~0.00–0.01 vs noise 0.60–0.85 measured) refuses noise-family audio outright. Retest (synthetic ground truth): silence/click/white noise/pink noise/vinyl hiss/drums-only all read Unread with no tonal claim in prompts; A-minor/G-minor/C-major loops read their true keys (clear); 120 BPM reads 120.1 (high confidence); 6-minute file onset rate 2.00/s. Known residual, labeled an estimate in the UI as before: a fifth-led E-minor synthetic reads F major (not clear), and an equal-weight Am–F–C–G pad reads F major (clear) — relative-major ambiguity on genuinely ambiguous material, not fabrication.

